{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The STDO Axiomatic Calculus for Governed Symbolic Systems, a_c: a domain-specific, carrier-neutral constitutional calculus for representing and relating governed symbolic systems. Stable concept identity: urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:a-c; owning bounded context: urn:stdo:bounded-context:axiomatic-calculus. Its separately identifiable specialization defines sorts, objects, relations, constraints, latitude, residuals, traversals, functor kinds, judgments, closure, projections, transformations, models and validity laws.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1,
          "quote": "# The STDO Axiomatic Calculus for Governed Symbolic Systems",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems",
          "start_line": 1
        },
        {
          "end_line": 7,
          "quote": "The **STDO Axiomatic Calculus for Governed Symbolic Systems**, written `a_c`,\nis STDO's domain-specific, carrier-neutral constitutional calculus for\nrepresenting and relating governed symbolic systems.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FPosition",
          "start_line": 5
        }
      ],
      "key": "axiomatic_calculus",
      "label": "Subject calculus a_c"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Historical derivation clause families: IDENTITY_METHOD core-law and authority-identity-and-conservation-stdo-up-004 supply scoped identity and authority conservation; SPEC_METHOD bounded-context-semantic-resolution supplies exact contextual meaning and cross-context relations; REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD position/evaluation motivate bounded probabilistic construction; SPEC_METHOD agentic-construction-execution-stdo-up-020 motivates delegated construction, exact candidates and construction/assessment separation; ambiguity-governance-rule motivates governed ambiguity, bounded processing, human adjudication and hard stops; REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD rf-005, rf-006, rf-007 and rf-012 motivate exact basis, authority conservation, evidence/verdict separation and closed results; SPEC_METHOD constitutional-chain motivates derivation/re-entry; REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD reference-frame-laws motivate finite closure, projection, authority, residuals and capability fit; SPEC_METHOD one-constitutional-surface-and-version-boundary-stdo-surface-001 motivates singular released authority. These are provenance references, not supplied definitions of those external laws.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 75,
          "quote": "The following clause families document the design provenance of the calculus:\n\n- scoped identity and authority conservation from\n  [`IDENTITY_METHOD.md#core-law`](IDENTITY_METHOD.md#core-law) and\n  [`IDENTITY_METHOD.md#authority-identity-and-conservation-stdo-up-004`](IDENTITY_METHOD.md#authority-identity-and-conservation-stdo-up-004);",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FDerivation%20Provenance",
          "start_line": 71
        }
      ],
      "key": "derivation_provenance",
      "label": "Derivation provenance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Sigma = (RecordKind, Sort, RelationKind, ConstraintKind, ResidualKind, FunctorKind, JudgmentKind, StopKind). Every member is a finite closed identity set for one model family. RecordKind_ac consists of the eight identities with prefix urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind: and suffixes semantic-object, typed-relation, constraint, latitude, residual, traversal, transformation and judgment. RefDomain_Sigma(record_kind, field) returns cardinality, allowed local record kinds, allowed semantic-object sorts, allowed external target kinds and required basis relation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 159,
          "quote": "Sigma = (\n  RecordKind,\n  Sort,\n  RelationKind,\n  ConstraintKind,\n  ResidualKind,\n  FunctorKind,\n  JudgmentKind,\n  StopKind\n)",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FCore%20Signature",
          "start_line": 150
        }
      ],
      "key": "calculus_signature",
      "label": "Closed calculus signature"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "M_b = (b, I, O, E, C, L, X, V, T, J): exact basis b, closed identity universe I, and finite populations of semantic objects O, typed directed relations E, constitutional constraints C, declared latitude L, residual uncertainty X, traversals V, transformations T and judgments J. Population_M maps the eight core record-kind identities to those populations. Local_M is their disjoint union; I = Local_M disjoint_union External_M.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 211,
          "quote": "M_b = (b, I, O, E, C, L, X, V, T, J)\n\nPopulation_M = {\n  urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind:semantic-object  -> O,\n  urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind:typed-relation  -> E,\n  urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind:constraint      -> C,\n  urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind:latitude        -> L,\n  urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind:residual        -> X,\n  urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind:traversal       -> V,\n  urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind:transformation  -> T,\n  urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:record-kind:judgment        -> J",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FCore%20Signature",
          "start_line": 201
        }
      ],
      "key": "calculus_model",
      "label": "Exact-basis calculus model"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "SemanticObject has exactly id, sort, context, owner, scope, basis and value. id, sort, context, owner and basis are identities; scope is the closed governed domain; value is sort-defined carrier-neutral content or a reference to exact content.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 286,
          "quote": "SemanticObject = {\n  id,\n  sort,\n  context,\n  owner,\n  scope,\n  basis,\n  value\n}",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FSemantic%20Object",
          "start_line": 278
        }
      ],
      "key": "semantic_object",
      "label": "SemanticObject record contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "TypedRelation has exactly id, kind, source, target, context, owner, scope, basis and qualifiers. source and target resolve to I. qualifiers is a closed kind-specific record containing direction, cardinality, preservation, loss, inverse, invalidation or other relation semantics required by the signature.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 313,
          "quote": "`source` and `target` resolve to members of `I`. `qualifiers` is the closed\nkind-specific record declaring direction, cardinality, preservation, loss,\ninverse, invalidation, or other relation semantics required by the signature.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FTyped%20Relation",
          "start_line": 311
        }
      ],
      "key": "typed_relation",
      "label": "TypedRelation record contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Constraint has exactly id, kind, applies_to, predicate, context, owner, scope, basis, judgment_kind, latitude_ref and refusal. kind resolves one declared ConstraintKind; judgment_kind resolves one declared JudgmentKind. The owning signature defines its predicate language and result judgment.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 332,
          "quote": "Constraint = {\n  id,\n  kind,\n  applies_to,\n  predicate,\n  context,\n  owner,\n  scope,\n  basis,\n  judgment_kind,\n  latitude_ref,\n  refusal\n}",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FConstraint",
          "start_line": 320
        }
      ],
      "key": "constraint_record",
      "label": "Constraint record contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Latitude is an explicit permission set within a constraint boundary, with fields id, applies_to, allowed_variation, forbidden_variation, context, owner, scope, basis and invalidation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 356,
          "quote": "Latitude = {\n  id,\n  applies_to,\n  allowed_variation,\n  forbidden_variation,\n  context,\n  owner,\n  scope,\n  basis,\n  invalidation\n}",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FLatitude",
          "start_line": 346
        }
      ],
      "key": "latitude_record",
      "label": "Latitude record contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Residual uncertainty is first-class model content. Residual fields are id, subject, kind, uncertainty, consequence, context, owner, scope, basis, re_entry and invalidation. kind resolves one declared ResidualKind; the owning signature defines lawful subject, uncertainty, consequence, re-entry and invalidation domains.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 379,
          "quote": "Residual = {\n  id,\n  subject,\n  kind,\n  uncertainty,\n  consequence,\n  context,\n  owner,\n  scope,\n  basis,\n  re_entry,\n  invalidation\n}",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTyped%20Records%2FResidual",
          "start_line": 367
        }
      ],
      "key": "residual_record",
      "label": "Residual record contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Material closure for M, seeds Z and exact dependency family R is closure_R(M, Z) = lfp(lambda Q: Z union required_R(M, Q)). It denotes least lawful record closure under R, without claiming minimum prose, bytes, tokens, cost or cognitive effort.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 524,
          "quote": "closure_R(M, Z) = lfp(lambda Q: Z union required_R(M, Q))",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-010%20Material%20Closure",
          "start_line": 524
        }
      ],
      "key": "material_closure",
      "label": "Material closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Projection selects an exact view without changing selected meaning for a declared consumer task: project(M, Z, R) -> (M_Z, Boundary_Z) | gap | refusal. M_Z contains material closure; Boundary_Z records excluded, external, unresolved, translated and invalidation-sensitive seams.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 544,
          "quote": "`M_Z` contains AC-010's material closure of `Z` under `R`. `Boundary_Z` records\nexcluded, external, unresolved, translated and invalidation-sensitive seams.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-011%20Projection",
          "start_line": 543
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_projection",
      "label": "Model projection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An overlay adds explicit records to one or more declared Population_M families of a basis model.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 551,
          "quote": "An overlay adds explicit records to one or more declared `Population_M`\nfamilies of a basis model.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-012%20Overlay",
          "start_line": 550
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_overlay",
      "label": "Model overlay"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Transformation t is an operation-bearing specialization of one exact traversal. Its fields are id, traversal, domain_model, codomain_model, context, owner, scope, basis, operation_authority, preconditions, preservation_relation, preserved, introduced, removed, external_preserved, external_introduced, external_removed, external_resolution_witnesses, residuals, evidence, provenance, stop_states, invalidation and re_entry. Application is T[t](M_b, inputs) -> M_b_prime* | hold | gap | refusal, where * denotes a proposed successor. Resolution_M(x) has exact identity-bearing fields external_identity, reference_domain, external_target_kind, resolved_target_identity, basis_relation, resolution_basis and evidence_identity; ResolutionSet_M contains these tuples for External_M.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 559,
          "quote": "A transformation is an operation-bearing specialization of one exact traversal\nrecord `v`:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-013%20Transformation",
          "start_line": 558
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_transformation",
      "label": "Transformation and external resolution contracts"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Judgment points to an unchanged subject. Its required semantic fields are id, kind, subject, subject_digest, context, owner, scope, basis, evaluator, authority, predicate, verdict, decision, performance, effects, evidence, residuals, provenance and decided_at. decision retains the original calculus/domain outcome rather than defining another verdict enumeration; predicate, verdict, performance, effects, evidence and residuals bind the shared result contract.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 759,
          "quote": "Judgment = {\n  id,\n  kind,\n  subject,\n  subject_digest,\n  context,\n  owner,\n  scope,\n  basis,\n  evaluator,\n  authority,\n  predicate,\n  verdict,\n  decision,\n  performance,\n  effects,\n  evidence,\n  residuals,\n  provenance,\n  decided_at\n}",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws%2FAC-014%20Judgment%20Separation",
          "start_line": 739
        }
      ],
      "key": "judgment_contract",
      "label": "Judgment contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Traversal v is a typed contract with id, domain, codomain, context, owner, scope, basis, preconditions, postconditions, authority, evidence, provenance and stop_states. F_K[v](X_v) -> Y_v | Omega_v binds a functor kind, exact traversal identity, typed input/result domains and closed stop refinement. F_D = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-d classifies deterministic evaluation/proof returning a judgment without performing the construction judged. F_P = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-p classifies bounded probabilistic interpretation/construction/proposal. F_H = urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:f-h classifies explicit human adjudication under exact identity, grant, subject, basis and decision contract.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 851,
          "quote": "- `F_D` classifies deterministic evaluation or proof over declared properties.\n  It returns a judgment and does not perform the domain construction being\n  judged.\n- `F_P` classifies bounded probabilistic interpretation, construction, or\n  proposal under an exact traversal contract. Its result has no semantic,\n  admission, acceptance, or closure authority merely because it is useful or\n  repeatable.\n- `F_H` classifies explicit human adjudication under an exact identity,\n  authority grant, subject, basis, and decision contract. Human presence grants\n  no ambient authority.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FTraversals%20And%20Functor%20Kinds",
          "start_line": 842
        }
      ],
      "key": "traversal_contract",
      "label": "Traversal and functor-kind contracts"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Interpret_a_c(subject_basis, signature, interpretation_contract) -> M_subject* | hold | gap | refusal applies the calculus to an exact subject. M_subject* is a proposed model lacking the applicable semantic acceptance judgment. a_c.X denotes the interpreted-subject layer.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 920,
          "quote": "Interpret_a_c(subject_basis, signature, interpretation_contract)\n  -> M_subject* | hold | gap | refusal\n```\n\nThe trailing `*` marks a proposed model that has not received the applicable\nsemantic acceptance judgment.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FInterpretation%20And%20Models",
          "start_line": 915
        }
      ],
      "key": "subject_interpretation",
      "label": "Subject interpretation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "a_c.X.C is the accepted interpreted model encoded in carrier C. The described relations are M_X = Interpret_a_c(X); J_X = Accept_X(M_X) -> accepted | refuse; G_C = Encode_C(M_X, J_X) where J_X = accepted; D_C = F_D[v_carrier_admission](G_C, Profile_C, CarrierBasis_C) -> admitted | refuse. J_X is external semantic acceptance; D_C judges unchanged carrier bytes. The admitted encoding evidence is the exact triple (G_C, J_X, D_C).",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1050,
          "quote": "M_X = Interpret_a_c(X)\nJ_X = Accept_X(M_X) -> accepted | refuse\nG_C = Encode_C(M_X, J_X) where J_X = accepted\nD_C = F_D[v_carrier_admission](G_C, Profile_C, CarrierBasis_C)\n      -> admitted | refuse",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FSubject%20And%20Carrier%20Boundaries",
          "start_line": 1046
        }
      ],
      "key": "encoded_carrier",
      "label": "Encoded-carrier layer"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "AxiomaticCalculusBasis is an external record created after publication bytes exist, distinguishing predecessor derivation authority from successor publication. It has kind stdo.axiomatic-calculus-basis, schema_version 1, concept_identity urn:stdo:concept:axiomatic-calculus:a-c; derivation_basis containing release_uri, manifest_sha256 and nonempty principle_refs; publication_basis containing release_uri, manifest_sha256, member_uri and member_sha256. Release URIs are absolute immutable predecessor/successor URIs respectively; principle references are absolute semantic addresses; member_uri is absolute; digest values are 'sha256:' followed by 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1081,
          "quote": "The stable concept identity does not identify one immutable calculus edition.\nAn exact released calculus basis is an external record created after the\npublication bytes exist. It keeps the predecessor authority from which the\nedition was derived distinct from the successor carrier that publishes it:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FExact%20Calculus%20Identity",
          "start_line": 1078
        }
      ],
      "key": "exact_calculus_basis",
      "label": "Exact released calculus basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Design Module Method owns generic design-contract law and an adoptable realization profile. Its purpose is stronger implementation discipline, with biases toward low coupling, modular composition, immutable typed carriers, total or nearly-total semantic transforms and explicit effect boundaries. Goals include reduced ambiguity, entropy, avoidable debt, hidden authority/mutation and semantic drift; increased refactorability; and evidence enabling later audit and reconstruction. It concerns realization design and implementation shape, not language ideology.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1,
          "quote": "# Design Module Method",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method",
          "start_line": 1
        },
        {
          "end_line": 60,
          "quote": "- low coupling\n- modular composition\n- immutable typed carriers\n- total or nearly-total semantic transforms\n- explicit effect boundaries",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F1.%20Purpose",
          "start_line": 56
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_module_method",
      "label": "Design Module Method"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A governed design boundary is the scoped unit to which selected design relations apply. A material semantic boundary changes identity, authority, lifecycle, public contract, externally observable Product meaning, cross-module topology, or accepted effect/closure law. Module boundaries are one realization-level means of bounded intermediate reasoning, implementation, evaluation, proof and later refactoring; schemas/carrier families, entity/record design and interface contracts are also within the method's scope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 421,
          "quote": "Proportionality applies. A **material semantic boundary** is one whose change\naffects identity, authority, lifecycle, public contract, externally observable\nProduct meaning, cross-module topology, or accepted effect or closure law.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule",
          "start_line": 419
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_boundary",
      "label": "Governed design boundary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Ontology is prior semantic-design authority for an adopted boundary, subordinate to constitutional WHAT. It derives from intent, Product, requirements and applicable graph/domain method and, once accepted, governs entities, relationships, invariants, lifecycle, authority, functions, composition and effects. Its inventory includes entities, identities, relationships, invariants, lifecycle, authority, atomic functions, higher-order composition, effects and projections. Accepted design separately owns structural HOW: topology, interfaces, carrier placement, module boundaries, algorithms and local realization.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 406,
          "quote": "The Ontology does not replace target architecture or module design. Accepted\ndesign remains the structural `HOW` for topology, interfaces, carrier placement,\nmodule boundaries, algorithms, and local realization. Implementation and tests\nderive from requirements plus accepted design and must preserve the accepted\nOntology; they are not direct substitutes for either layer.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule",
          "start_line": 402
        },
        {
          "end_line": 517,
