    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Foundation selection is a proportional, bounded, reproducible, explicitly non-exhaustive comparison of candidate compositions for material generic capabilities, common mechanics, algorithms and dependencies. Candidates may draw on admitted project foundations, language/runtime/platform/standard-library capabilities, accepted predecessor or immutable-lineage evidence, maintained external foundations where dependencies are permitted, and bounded local implementations. The undominated frontier contains lawful materially incomparable candidate compositions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 633,
          "quote": "Native constructability proves that the selected substrate can realize the\ndesign. It does not prove that locally constructing another generic mechanism\nis the best realization. Before constructing a material generic capability,\ncommon mechanic, or algorithm, or selecting a material dependency, the active\ndesign boundary must perform a proportional foundation-selection pass.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FCapability%20And%20Foundation%20Selection%20%28%60STDO-UP-023%60%29",
