      "label": "Complete transformation-admission relation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Atomic function families are the smallest parameterized families making entity lifecycles logically complete. Function-derivation evidence records discovered functionality, entity, atomic function/template, higher-order composition, effect class, required authority and disposition. Higher-order behavior has explicit composition/effect algebra; examples include unit/lift, sequential, parallel/applicative, fold/evaluation, retry/recovery, recursion/fixed point and projection.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 922,
          "quote": "The Ontology must derive the smallest parameterized atomic function families\nthat make the entity lifecycles logically complete. Variation that changes only\nan entity kind, projection kind, transition kind, policy, or subordinate payload\nshould normally be a typed parameter or closed variant of one function family,\nnot another peer function.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FAtomic%20Functions%20And%20Higher-Order%20Composition",
