          "end_line": 398,
          "quote": "These arguments apply [F1–F6](FOUNDATIONS.md#foundational-commitments)\nto a governed symbolic model under a declared signature and interpretation.\nThe model's records represent a separately identified subject; some predicates\nadmit mechanical decision and others require semantic judgment. These are the\ncalculus's instance conditions. Its finite signature and record choices do not\nfollow from an actor's finite capacity. Selection authority remains bound by\n[Interpretation And Models](#interpretation-and-models).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md#The%20STDO%20Axiomatic%20Calculus%20for%20Governed%20Symbolic%20Systems%2FFundamental%20Laws",
          "start_line": 392
        }
      ],
      "key": "fundamental_law_argument_boundaries",
      "statement": "The family arguments are conditional: recoverable referents and typed composition support admission; scoped identity, evidence and grants distinguish identity/authority/basis; constraint evaluation preserves predicate, cause, performance and effects; uncertainty gives neither permission nor disposition authority; task views retain exact dependencies and external seams; producing successors does not admit them; shared executors do not turn proposals into assessment/adjudication; structural validity does not establish fidelity; evidence at one layer does not grant another layer or Product standing. Owners retain concrete sorts/reference domains, identity/composite bases, domain predicates, permitted variation/residual dispositions, seeds/required-edge semantics, equality/loss/grants, topology/operator, material selection/admitted loss, and Product acceptance/release. Closed syntax does not prove semantic adequacy; finite capacity proves neither chosen inventories nor finite unique closure or minimum cost. Calculus policies are additional choices, including total populations, exact bases, cause-sensitive judgments, explicit L/X, least fixed points/Boundary_Z, overlays/deltas, three functor kinds/bracket syntax, source treatment dispositions and separate layer identities.",
