          "start_line": 339
        }
      ],
      "key": "python_boundary_typing",
      "statement": "All repo-authored functions/methods/callables, including tests/scripts/support, require explicit parameter/return annotations and closed typed semantic carriers. Untyped def, implicit Any, semantic dict[str, Any]/Mapping[str, Any]/object and unchecked JSON passed across layers through .get chains do not count; semantic-center dict[str, Any] is design debt. Use closed forms such as dataclass/TypedDict/Protocol/Literal/Enum; validate dynamic input and narrow before transforms; Any stays at foreign ingress and collapses immediately. Run a strict static pass such as mypy --strict or equivalent; a boundary that cannot pass cleanly is not implementation-conformant.",
      "uncertainty": ""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["design_ontology", "design_boundary"],
      "conditions": ["Ontology-first profile applies to a new or materially changed active semantic boundary."],
      "exceptions": ["A realization_refactor wholly within accepted boundary may cite existing Ontology/design and prove no ontology delta without reauthoring/reaccepting unchanged evidence."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 394,
          "quote": "Every new or materially changed active semantic boundary must derive its\nsemantic model from one accepted **Ontology**. Before a new or changed entity,\nfunction, authority, effect, Prime-conformant carrier, module boundary, public\noperation, or schema is promoted, the affected boundary must first produce a\ncandidate Ontology slice and carry it through the acceptance sequence below.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule",
          "start_line": 390
        }
      ],
      "key": "ontology_first_acceptance",
      "statement": "Derive semantic model from one accepted Ontology; before promotion of changed/new entity, function, authority, effect, Prime-conformant carrier, module boundary, public operation or schema, produce candidate slice through acceptance sequence. Ontology derives from constitutional sources without inventing Product meaning; implementation/tests derive from requirements plus accepted design and preserve it. Acceptance identifies constitutional sources, accepting design authority, stable element/relation identities and version/basis making staleness and affected projection set decidable. Repricing invalidates only affected relations/projections until reevaluated; code/tests/generated schemas/single implementer cannot self-ratify authority. Acceptance may share the three-view design pack/record, requiring no second document/ticket/ceremony. Semantic/structural change publishes affected delta and impact set only; unrelated work does not freeze on one changed digest.",
      "uncertainty": "Later acceptance-sequence details and three-view gate are not fully supplied."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["material_design_network", "design_boundary"],
      "conditions": ["Sequencing work on a governed boundary."],
      "exceptions": ["A disposable spike may supply evidence across unresolved decisions without becoming selected design."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 459,
          "quote": "- **co-evolution** is lawful only when `decision_complete(B)` holds. Design\n  still owns the structural `HOW`, but design, implementation, and tests may\n  evolve together and reconcile before promotion or closure;\n- **design-gated** sequencing is required when implementation would otherwise\n  establish an unresolved or contradictory relation, or choose between\n  materially non-equivalent networks, with durable consequences for future\n  change. The affected design decision must be accepted before retained\n  implementation establishes it. A disposable spike may supply evidence\n  without becoming the selected design.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FProportional%20Design%20Sequencing",
          "start_line": 451
        }
      ],
      "key": "proportional_sequencing_modes",
      "statement": "Neither mode is globally default. Co-evolution requires decision completeness and reconciles design/implementation/tests before promotion/closure while design owns HOW. Design gate is required before retained implementation establishes unresolved/contradictory relations or selects materially nonequivalent networks with durable consequences. Proportionality selects material relations and smallest causally closed affected gate; decision completeness tests admissibility. Reduced downstream code/runtime/test/review/reconciliation space is counterfactual contraction evidence, not an independent gate reason. Prior gate is warranted when ambiguity contraction justifies reasoning load; absent divergent architecture a separate gate adds no disambiguation. Bidirectional design/implementation is evidence only: code may expose counterexamples/ambiguity, not choose missing meaning, rewrite upstream truth or retroactively ratify decisions. Record selected relation/basis in existing carrier. Hidden ambiguity switches affected set to gated mode without invalidating unrelated work; repairs do not establish acceptance. Repeated/cross-cutting surprises enter Universal Intake Triage. Only diagnosis of underconstrained design with sufficient requirements requires bounded design_reframe; retained affected code stays provisional until acceptance. Surprise alone does not establish design cause; retain alternatives and select upstream change/further observation before treating design gate as sufficient.",
      "uncertainty": "Universal Intake Triage's full diagnosis procedure is external."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["material_design_network", "design_boundary"],
      "conditions": ["Decision completeness or promotion is claimed."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 566,
          "quote": "The boundary is underconstrained whenever `decision_complete(B)` is false,\nincluding when implementation must introduce a new material semantic decision,\nprojections contradict one another, or two materially non-equivalent networks\nremain admissible. Local algorithm and layout choices may remain open only when\nevery admitted choice is semantically equivalent under the accepted network.\nContradictory projections make the design unsatisfiable and block promotion.\n\nDecision completeness does not require another document. The constraint\nnetwork may be expressed through the existing Ontology, algebra, module mapping,\nand views when those surfaces jointly carry the complete relation.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD.md#Design%20Module%20Method%2F4B.%20Ontology-First%20Design%20Rule%2FDecision-Complete%20Symbolic%20Design",
          "start_line": 557
        }
      ],
      "key": "decision_completeness_evidence",
      "statement": "Ontology, Prime contraction, IACS, design, domain/sequence/state views, contracts, modules, implementation and tests project one M(B) network. Their presence, headings, plausibility or mechanical validity do not prove decision completeness. A false decision_complete predicate means underconstrained, including missing implementation-made semantic decisions, contradictory projections or nonequivalent admitted networks. Contradictions make design unsatisfiable and block promotion. Algorithm/layout choices remain open only when all admitted choices are semantically equivalent. Existing Ontology/algebra/mapping/views may jointly express network; no extra document is required.",
      "uncertainty": ""
